00:01:13.819 --> 00:01:15.610
And so what I'm going
to try to cover today

00:01:15.610 --> 00:01:19.870
is a brief history of
Spanner, why it was created.

00:01:19.870 --> 00:01:22.220
What was the
motivation at Google?

00:01:22.220 --> 00:01:25.312
What is Cloud Spanner as
it has been released today?

00:01:25.312 --> 00:01:26.020
How does it work?

00:01:26.020 --> 00:01:27.520
How does it compare
to other things?

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:31.780
And then, finally, a
brief demo that we better

00:01:31.780 --> 00:01:34.660
get to considering all the
work that went into it.

00:01:34.660 --> 00:01:35.800
So why does Spanner exist?

00:01:35.800 --> 00:01:40.510
And to get to that you really
have to go back to about 2005.

00:01:40.510 --> 00:01:44.260
At that time pretty much every
dollar that Google generated

00:01:44.260 --> 00:01:46.810
was based off of AdWords.

00:01:46.810 --> 00:01:50.290
And AdWords was backed by
a manually sharded MySQL

00:01:50.290 --> 00:01:51.310
database.

00:01:51.310 --> 00:01:54.620
And that database was
sharded on customer keys--

00:01:54.620 --> 00:01:56.140
or customer IDs, rather.

00:01:56.140 --> 00:01:58.669
And, as we grew we had
to reshard that database.

00:01:58.669 --> 00:02:01.210
And we had to reshard it a lot
because we were growing really

00:02:01.210 --> 00:02:02.650
quickly.

00:02:02.650 --> 00:02:05.911
And the reshards were
getting increasingly painful,

00:02:05.911 --> 00:02:08.410
to the point where the last one
actually took several years,

00:02:08.410 --> 00:02:10.419
literally, to complete.

00:02:10.419 --> 00:02:13.435
At that point we said, you
know, this is untenable.

00:02:13.435 --> 00:02:14.560
We can't continue this way.

00:02:14.560 --> 00:02:16.490
There has to be
something better.

00:02:16.490 --> 00:02:19.810
And so something better led
to essentially the development

00:02:19.810 --> 00:02:21.260
of Spanner.

00:02:21.260 --> 00:02:23.080
And so while we
were planning out

00:02:23.080 --> 00:02:24.790
what eventually
became Spanner, we

00:02:24.790 --> 00:02:28.049
had to sort through what
our requirements were.

00:02:28.049 --> 00:02:29.590
And, essentially,
at that time, those

00:02:29.590 --> 00:02:31.480
were, what were
AdWords' requirements?

00:02:31.480 --> 00:02:33.220
And so there were
three big ones.

00:02:33.220 --> 00:02:35.439
First, it had to be a
horizontally scalable database.

00:02:35.439 --> 00:02:37.480
This was the problem we
were encountering already

00:02:37.480 --> 00:02:38.470
with MySQL.

00:02:38.470 --> 00:02:39.980
We had to solve for that.

00:02:39.980 --> 00:02:42.640
And so we needed something
that could scale with us.

00:02:42.640 --> 00:02:46.480
We had gone from a $10 million a
year company to over $1 billion

00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:49.090
a year in such a short time.

00:02:49.090 --> 00:02:50.747
And we knew the
scale wasn't ending.

00:02:50.747 --> 00:02:52.330
It was only going
to be more and more.

00:02:52.330 --> 00:02:54.550
And so we had to be
horizontally scalable.

00:02:54.550 --> 00:02:57.370
Second, we had to have
asset transactions.

00:02:57.370 --> 00:03:01.930
We were dealing with money,
budgets, customers, partners,

00:03:01.930 --> 00:03:02.980
agencies.

00:03:02.980 --> 00:03:05.020
We had to be sure that
any transaction that

00:03:05.020 --> 00:03:08.230
got written to the database
was a strong transaction.

00:03:08.230 --> 00:03:10.870
We couldn't deal with
eventual consistency,

00:03:10.870 --> 00:03:12.080
we couldn't accept data loss.

00:03:12.080 --> 00:03:15.670
We had to have asset
transactions in consistency.

00:03:15.670 --> 00:03:18.840
And then finally, we
couldn't afford downtime.

00:03:18.840 --> 00:03:23.380
AdWords was the thing that
was driving all of Google

00:03:23.380 --> 00:03:24.130
at the time.

00:03:24.130 --> 00:03:26.920
So every minute, every second
that AdWords was down meant

00:03:26.920 --> 00:03:28.580
lost revenue to Google.

00:03:28.580 --> 00:03:31.060
And so downtime was
unacceptable for any system

00:03:31.060 --> 00:03:33.770
that we were creating to
replace the existing one.

00:03:33.770 --> 00:03:36.480
So we had to have up time.

00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:39.122
And so if you look at
these three requirements

00:03:39.122 --> 00:03:41.080
and think about what was
available at the time,

00:03:41.080 --> 00:03:43.600
there was really
nothing that could

00:03:43.600 --> 00:03:48.430
fit those requirements in the
market until Cloud Spanner.

00:03:48.430 --> 00:03:51.257
And so that brings us to
what Cloud Spanner is.

00:03:51.257 --> 00:03:53.090
And if you could play
the video now, please.

00:03:56.834 --> 00:03:58.710
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:03:58.710 --> 00:04:00.990
- We demand a lot
of our databases,

00:04:00.990 --> 00:04:03.810
and our databases
demand a lot of us.

00:04:03.810 --> 00:04:07.320
They're fragile, expensive,
and tough to maintain.

00:04:07.320 --> 00:04:09.780
When things break, it can be
hard to put the pieces back

00:04:09.780 --> 00:04:12.060
together at the speed
of your business,

00:04:12.060 --> 00:04:14.980
and you often lose
data in the process.

00:04:14.980 --> 00:04:17.760
We've all come to expect that
distributed databases can't

00:04:17.760 --> 00:04:20.760
be globally consistent
and scalable.

00:04:20.760 --> 00:04:23.190
But what if you didn't
have to make tradeoffs?

00:04:23.190 --> 00:04:25.770
What if you could have a
fully managed database service

00:04:25.770 --> 00:04:27.930
that's consistent,
scales horizontally

00:04:27.930 --> 00:04:30.960
across data centers,
and speaks SQL?

00:04:30.960 --> 00:04:34.650
This is Cloud Spanner, a
mission-critical relational

00:04:34.650 --> 00:04:36.990
database service built
from the ground up

00:04:36.990 --> 00:04:39.870
and battle-tested at Google
for strong consistency

00:04:39.870 --> 00:04:42.930
and high availability
at global scale.

00:04:42.930 --> 00:04:45.000
At Google we use
Spanner for services

00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:48.030
that billions of people
access every day.

00:04:48.030 --> 00:04:49.950
It's the only cloud
service designed

00:04:49.950 --> 00:04:52.080
to be a massively
distributed relational

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:56.530
database with transactions
and SQL semantics.

00:04:56.530 --> 00:04:58.380
And we're talking massive.

00:04:58.380 --> 00:05:01.560
Spanner can scale up to millions
of machines across hundreds

00:05:01.560 --> 00:05:04.620
of data centers and trillions
of database rows stretching

00:05:04.620 --> 00:05:07.650
across the globe while
behaving like it's all in one

00:05:07.650 --> 00:05:10.500
place, where you want it.

00:05:10.500 --> 00:05:13.740
We've built Cloud Spanner to
be fully managed and secure.

00:05:13.740 --> 00:05:16.200
Automatic sharding and
synchronous replication

00:05:16.200 --> 00:05:19.440
with low latency and schema
updates without downtime

00:05:19.440 --> 00:05:23.010
means your data is highly
available and reliable.

00:05:23.010 --> 00:05:26.100
Instead of endless provisioning
and worrying about maintenance,

00:05:26.100 --> 00:05:29.340
you can focus on
growing your business.

00:05:29.340 --> 00:05:31.200
No matter your
needs, you don't have

00:05:31.200 --> 00:05:33.470
to hit a wall with
your database service.

00:05:33.470 --> 00:05:38.420
Get started with Cloud
Spanner and build what's next.

00:05:38.420 --> 00:05:41.639
[END PLAYBACK]

00:05:41.639 --> 00:05:42.930
PETER-MARK VERWOERD: All right.

00:05:42.930 --> 00:05:44.460
So I'm just here to
introduce that video.

00:05:44.460 --> 00:05:46.130
Thank you very much
everyone, goodbye--

00:05:46.130 --> 00:05:47.630
kidding.

00:05:47.630 --> 00:05:52.310
So that's just an overview
of what Cloud Spanner does.

00:05:52.310 --> 00:05:54.480
And when you dive into
it, what is Cloud Spanner?

00:05:54.480 --> 00:05:56.780
And how does that
relate to what Spanner

00:05:56.780 --> 00:05:59.420
was as an internal product?

00:05:59.420 --> 00:06:03.170
So first of all, Spanner is a
fully managed database service,

00:06:03.170 --> 00:06:06.020
which means that you don't
have to worry about upgrades,

00:06:06.020 --> 00:06:10.220
patching, security, keeping it
online, or anything like that.

00:06:10.220 --> 00:06:12.980
That's all done by our own
site reliability engineering

00:06:12.980 --> 00:06:16.280
team that makes sure
that Spanner is up 24/7.

00:06:19.130 --> 00:06:22.880
As you may imagine, we get
fairly pedantic at Google

00:06:22.880 --> 00:06:25.020
actually about a
number of things.

00:06:25.020 --> 00:06:27.800
But one of them is about
what does consistency mean?

00:06:27.800 --> 00:06:29.540
And what does global
consistency mean?

00:06:29.540 --> 00:06:33.830
And for us, it means
external serializability.

00:06:33.830 --> 00:06:35.600
And that's a highest
standard for what

00:06:35.600 --> 00:06:37.460
global consistency means.

00:06:37.460 --> 00:06:40.670
And there's a lot of
discussion inside the industry

00:06:40.670 --> 00:06:43.250
about eventual consistency
versus global, and what

00:06:43.250 --> 00:06:46.280
really that is versus
what's acceptable.

00:06:46.280 --> 00:06:48.170
For us, we had to
set the highest

00:06:48.170 --> 00:06:49.880
bar possible for
what our needs were,

00:06:49.880 --> 00:06:52.430
and Spanner meets that bar.

00:06:52.430 --> 00:06:55.760
And so that means that
you get asset transactions

00:06:55.760 --> 00:06:56.950
across many rows.

00:06:56.950 --> 00:06:58.250
You have relational semantics.

00:06:58.250 --> 00:07:01.460
You've got DDLs that
you can create tables.

00:07:01.460 --> 00:07:07.500
You can do inserts, updates,
deletes, queries using SQL.

00:07:07.500 --> 00:07:10.250
And, in fact, you
can use the same SQL

00:07:10.250 --> 00:07:12.750
that's already available
across Google Cloud

00:07:12.750 --> 00:07:14.760
Platform for BigQuery.

00:07:14.760 --> 00:07:17.914
So AntiSQL 2011
with some extensions

00:07:17.914 --> 00:07:20.330
is the same SQL that you use
in BigQuery that you can also

00:07:20.330 --> 00:07:23.930
use on Cloud Platform.

00:07:23.930 --> 00:07:27.950
We also have automated
synchronous replication.

00:07:27.950 --> 00:07:32.000
As of today, so we
have regional Spanner.

00:07:32.000 --> 00:07:34.310
So you can create regional
instances of Spanner.

00:07:34.310 --> 00:07:36.620
And those are within
a single region

00:07:36.620 --> 00:07:38.180
spread across many zones.

00:07:38.180 --> 00:07:40.850
And that gives you four
nines of availability.

00:07:40.850 --> 00:07:43.754
And so when you
write to Spanner,

00:07:43.754 --> 00:07:45.920
no matter where you're
writing it within the region,

00:07:45.920 --> 00:07:50.030
you know that write is
accepted and committed.

00:07:50.030 --> 00:07:53.930
And later this year
we will be rolling out

00:07:53.930 --> 00:07:56.000
multi-regional Spanner
which actually gives you

00:07:56.000 --> 00:07:57.830
a higher level of availability.

00:07:57.830 --> 00:07:59.870
That gives you five
nines of availability,

00:07:59.870 --> 00:08:03.840
which is about two seconds
of downtime per month.

00:08:03.840 --> 00:08:06.090
And so that's going to be
coming later this year.

00:08:06.090 --> 00:08:08.400
And finally, we've been
using this internally

00:08:08.400 --> 00:08:09.650
at Google for nearly 10 years.

00:08:09.650 --> 00:08:10.970
This is battle-tested.

00:08:10.970 --> 00:08:14.520
This has been run on production
systems for a long time.

00:08:14.520 --> 00:08:16.940
And the reason I can get up
here and make these claims

00:08:16.940 --> 00:08:19.040
about consistency,
about availability

00:08:19.040 --> 00:08:21.230
is because we've been
doing this for so long.

00:08:21.230 --> 00:08:23.300
We've got the data,
we've got the history

00:08:23.300 --> 00:08:26.300
to know that we can
stand by these claims.

00:08:26.300 --> 00:08:28.042
And in fact, in
addition to AdWords,

00:08:28.042 --> 00:08:30.500
which has been using Spanner
since it went into production,

00:08:30.500 --> 00:08:32.299
there are a lot of
other systems at Google

00:08:32.299 --> 00:08:33.470
that use Spanner as well.

00:08:33.470 --> 00:08:36.650
So the Play Store uses Spanner.

00:08:36.650 --> 00:08:39.890
Google Cloud Platform, in
fact, also uses Spanner.

00:08:39.890 --> 00:08:43.570
The data plane that
maintains all the VMs

00:08:43.570 --> 00:08:48.410
across Cloud Platform actually
runs on top of Spanner.

00:08:48.410 --> 00:08:51.770
So I just said a lot of
things about how it works

00:08:51.770 --> 00:08:53.510
and ways it doesn't work.

00:08:53.510 --> 00:08:56.090
But in a more concrete
sense, what does that mean?

00:08:56.090 --> 00:08:59.840
So I think it's useful to
compare it to both relational

00:08:59.840 --> 00:09:02.780
databases and NoSQL databases,
which is where we often

00:09:02.780 --> 00:09:04.040
get the most questions.

00:09:04.040 --> 00:09:07.070
So if you compare it to
a relational database,

00:09:07.070 --> 00:09:08.370
Spanner has SQL.

00:09:08.370 --> 00:09:09.860
Spanner does schema updates.

00:09:09.860 --> 00:09:13.310
It has strong consistency.

00:09:13.310 --> 00:09:15.260
And where the comparison
gets interesting

00:09:15.260 --> 00:09:17.150
is we often get
questions like, well,

00:09:17.150 --> 00:09:20.960
how does Spanner compare to my
RDBMS, MySQL [? plus plus, ?]

00:09:20.960 --> 00:09:22.850
whatever it is?

00:09:22.850 --> 00:09:26.570
And that's-- sometimes can be an
apples and oranges comparison.

00:09:26.570 --> 00:09:29.870
Because when you
talk about an RDBMS,

00:09:29.870 --> 00:09:33.920
you're usually talking
about a single node system

00:09:33.920 --> 00:09:40.760
where all the reads
and writes are

00:09:40.760 --> 00:09:43.940
going through a single
node, a particular node.

00:09:43.940 --> 00:09:45.800
And Spanner is a
distributed system.

00:09:45.800 --> 00:09:47.720
And so with a single
node system like

00:09:47.720 --> 00:09:52.760
that, if you've got any issues
like unexpected downtime,

00:09:52.760 --> 00:09:54.950
like unexpected
failure of a node,

00:09:54.950 --> 00:09:57.560
you have to do security patches,
you have to do an upgrade,

00:09:57.560 --> 00:09:59.280
you have downtime
with that system.

00:09:59.280 --> 00:10:01.190
And you can create
alternative strategies

00:10:01.190 --> 00:10:04.030
where you have things
like master-master-slaves,

00:10:04.030 --> 00:10:07.166
or read slaves
where you failover.

00:10:07.166 --> 00:10:09.290
And those alleviate the
downtime but there is still

00:10:09.290 --> 00:10:10.750
downtime that is incurred.

00:10:10.750 --> 00:10:13.682
And with Spanner, again, part
of our design requirements

00:10:13.682 --> 00:10:15.140
was that we couldn't
have downtime.

00:10:15.140 --> 00:10:17.631
And so there was that kind of--

00:10:17.631 --> 00:10:19.880
there's that kind of change
and that difference there.

00:10:22.590 --> 00:10:29.130
And so basically the strategy
for Spanner becomes--

00:10:29.130 --> 00:10:31.800
it's a horizontal
strategy for scaling.

00:10:31.800 --> 00:10:33.870
Meanwhile, with a
single node database

00:10:33.870 --> 00:10:36.012
your strategy is
vertical scaling.

00:10:36.012 --> 00:10:37.470
You go to say, I
need a bigger box.

00:10:37.470 --> 00:10:38.136
I need more RAM.

00:10:38.136 --> 00:10:40.564
I need more memory, more
disk, what have you.

00:10:40.564 --> 00:10:43.230
I hope the provider I have has a
bigger VM because we're hitting

00:10:43.230 --> 00:10:45.470
the limits on our database.

00:10:45.470 --> 00:10:47.250
With Spanner, that issue's gone.

00:10:47.250 --> 00:10:51.330
It doesn't have that
problem anymore.

00:10:51.330 --> 00:10:53.925
Now the other thing you can do
to scale relational databases

00:10:53.925 --> 00:10:55.350
is add read replicas.

00:10:55.350 --> 00:10:59.720
And so you alleviate that
load on top of the master node

00:10:59.720 --> 00:11:01.680
and spread the reads out
to the read replicas,

00:11:01.680 --> 00:11:03.600
you increase your capacity
of the master node.

00:11:03.600 --> 00:11:05.400
But then you have to deal
with replication lag.

00:11:05.400 --> 00:11:06.816
And so if you have
to be sure, you

00:11:06.816 --> 00:11:08.790
have to be positive
that what you're reading

00:11:08.790 --> 00:11:10.440
is the most consistent view.

00:11:10.440 --> 00:11:13.050
You can go back to the master
to get the most consistent view,

00:11:13.050 --> 00:11:15.240
but then you're consuming
the resources that you

00:11:15.240 --> 00:11:17.940
had set aside for writes.

00:11:17.940 --> 00:11:19.560
And so that's a
situation that just

00:11:19.560 --> 00:11:22.230
doesn't happen with Spanner.

00:11:22.230 --> 00:11:28.632
And on the other hand, with
non-relational databases,

00:11:28.632 --> 00:11:30.090
we often see the
situation where we

00:11:30.090 --> 00:11:34.320
go to customers, large and
small across the board, where

00:11:34.320 --> 00:11:36.270
we see a very familiar pattern.

00:11:36.270 --> 00:11:37.815
An application is created.

00:11:37.815 --> 00:11:40.970
It has a relational
database on the back end.

00:11:40.970 --> 00:11:42.720
The application grows
for whatever reason,

00:11:42.720 --> 00:11:43.600
becomes very popular.

00:11:43.600 --> 00:11:45.330
It starts throwing a
lot of data, and so on.

00:11:45.330 --> 00:11:47.079
At a certain point the
relational database

00:11:47.079 --> 00:11:49.340
is replaced by a
NoSQL database--

00:11:49.340 --> 00:11:52.440
Cassandra, MongoDB,
whatever it is.

00:11:52.440 --> 00:11:54.570
And when that
decision happens, it

00:11:54.570 --> 00:11:59.190
is usually done by saying our
top priority in this change

00:11:59.190 --> 00:12:00.960
is horizontal scalability.

00:12:00.960 --> 00:12:03.600
So we're willing to
sacrifice consistency

00:12:03.600 --> 00:12:05.139
if we can get that scalability.

00:12:05.139 --> 00:12:06.930
And so that trade off
is made, and then you

00:12:06.930 --> 00:12:09.120
start pushing all
this logic that

00:12:09.120 --> 00:12:11.400
once existed by the
nature of the database

00:12:11.400 --> 00:12:12.360
into the application.

00:12:12.360 --> 00:12:14.790
So you're starting
to create consistency

00:12:14.790 --> 00:12:17.040
within an application,
which is introducing

00:12:17.040 --> 00:12:20.370
more complexity, more
maintenance, more bugs

00:12:20.370 --> 00:12:22.840
for the sake of gaining
horizontal scalability.

00:12:22.840 --> 00:12:25.166
And with Spanner you don't
have to make that trade-off.

00:12:25.166 --> 00:12:26.790
You can continue to
scale horizontally,

00:12:26.790 --> 00:12:28.650
but you still have consistency.

00:12:28.650 --> 00:12:30.637
You still have
asset transactions.

00:12:34.120 --> 00:12:36.820
One of the things we
get asked a lot about

00:12:36.820 --> 00:12:40.060
is, what was the
hardest part of bringing

00:12:40.060 --> 00:12:44.380
Spanner from an internal
product to a Cloud product?

00:12:44.380 --> 00:12:47.260
And I think one of the things
that we worked hardest on

00:12:47.260 --> 00:12:48.880
was making it open.

00:12:48.880 --> 00:12:51.577
So Google is very much
about being an open cloud

00:12:51.577 --> 00:12:53.410
and meeting you where
you are with the tools

00:12:53.410 --> 00:12:54.580
that you already use.

00:12:54.580 --> 00:12:57.820
And so we tried to inject
this across the board

00:12:57.820 --> 00:12:59.530
into Cloud Spanner.

00:12:59.530 --> 00:13:03.310
So the first thing we started
with was using standard SQL.

00:13:03.310 --> 00:13:07.030
Like I said earlier, this
is the same SQL dialect

00:13:07.030 --> 00:13:09.099
that is spoken by
BigQuery right now.

00:13:09.099 --> 00:13:11.140
A couple of years ago, we
looked at our products.

00:13:11.140 --> 00:13:13.050
We said we were--

00:13:13.050 --> 00:13:16.300
honestly we were all over the
map with SQL standardization.

00:13:16.300 --> 00:13:20.590
So we decided to standardize
AntiSQL 2011 extensions.

00:13:20.590 --> 00:13:22.400
And that is currently
used by BigQuer.

00:13:22.400 --> 00:13:24.040
Now it's also used by Spanner.

00:13:24.040 --> 00:13:26.140
And so we wanted to make
sure we standardize that

00:13:26.140 --> 00:13:27.889
and made it a familiar
language for anyone

00:13:27.889 --> 00:13:29.740
who's making that transition.

00:13:29.740 --> 00:13:33.640
Second, we spent a lot of
time on enterprise features.

00:13:33.640 --> 00:13:35.920
So at launch--

00:13:35.920 --> 00:13:37.720
Spanner launched back in March--

00:13:37.720 --> 00:13:43.030
when it launched, it launched
with the most features

00:13:43.030 --> 00:13:45.040
of any of our new
products had ever

00:13:45.040 --> 00:13:47.920
launched with, things like
encryption, identity and access

00:13:47.920 --> 00:13:50.040
management logging, all
these sorts of things

00:13:50.040 --> 00:13:52.660
that any kind of
standard enterprise

00:13:52.660 --> 00:13:55.690
would be interested in
and be required to use

00:13:55.690 --> 00:13:57.010
a database in production.

00:13:57.010 --> 00:14:00.670
And Spanner launched
with those on day one.

00:14:00.670 --> 00:14:04.060
A third was to make
Spanner an appealing

00:14:04.060 --> 00:14:05.060
target for developers.

00:14:05.060 --> 00:14:07.300
We want to be a developer
friendly platform.

00:14:07.300 --> 00:14:08.890
And in order to
do that, we really

00:14:08.890 --> 00:14:10.951
think there are two
essential things.

00:14:10.951 --> 00:14:12.700
One, you have to have
great documentation.

00:14:12.700 --> 00:14:14.780
And two, you have to
have good libraries.

00:14:14.780 --> 00:14:17.830
And so for the
documentation, Spanner

00:14:17.830 --> 00:14:21.410
was actually in early
access for over 12 months.

00:14:21.410 --> 00:14:25.240
So we have an early access
program with some customers.

00:14:25.240 --> 00:14:27.370
And it was there
for over a year.

00:14:27.370 --> 00:14:30.040
And among the many things that
we did with our early access

00:14:30.040 --> 00:14:32.760
customers was actually
refine our documentation.

00:14:32.760 --> 00:14:35.080
They told us where the holes
were, where the gaps were,

00:14:35.080 --> 00:14:37.704
things didn't make sense, things
weren't explained well enough.

00:14:37.704 --> 00:14:40.360
And we iterated over, and over,
and over on that documentation.

00:14:40.360 --> 00:14:43.300
And it really helped a lot to be
able to make the documentation

00:14:43.300 --> 00:14:46.000
at launch really first class.

00:14:46.000 --> 00:14:50.260
Alongside that process was
developing client libraries.

00:14:50.260 --> 00:14:53.890
So we really wanted to launch
with a wide variety of popular

00:14:53.890 --> 00:14:58.180
platforms-- or popular
languages, I should say--

00:14:58.180 --> 00:14:59.680
with our libraries.

00:14:59.680 --> 00:15:03.289
And so we launched with Java
and Python and Node and Go.

00:15:03.289 --> 00:15:05.080
And those are really
first class libraries.

00:15:05.080 --> 00:15:07.690
What we've done
with those is tried

00:15:07.690 --> 00:15:09.640
to make it as easy as
possible for developers

00:15:09.640 --> 00:15:11.181
who are familiar
with those languages

00:15:11.181 --> 00:15:13.660
to get started and start
using Spanner in a way that's

00:15:13.660 --> 00:15:16.240
familiar, in a way that is
natural in that language.

00:15:16.240 --> 00:15:19.990
We've abstracted away a lot
of the subtle intricacies

00:15:19.990 --> 00:15:22.270
of connecting to Spanner,
of using the API,

00:15:22.270 --> 00:15:26.450
in a way that makes sense
for each particular language.

00:15:26.450 --> 00:15:28.450
And then finally we
launched with a JDBC driver.

00:15:28.450 --> 00:15:30.390
So we knew it was
important for Spanner

00:15:30.390 --> 00:15:32.330
to be able to connect
to existing tools,

00:15:32.330 --> 00:15:34.420
especially existing BI tools.

00:15:34.420 --> 00:15:37.720
And so we launched with a
JDBC driver that's read-only.

00:15:37.720 --> 00:15:41.027
And so what that means is
you can connect your Spanner

00:15:41.027 --> 00:15:43.360
instance to tools that you
already use, that you already

00:15:43.360 --> 00:15:45.580
have, and to be able to
read the data off of there.

00:15:45.580 --> 00:15:48.419
And so that's the kind of thing
that you can do for customers.

00:15:48.419 --> 00:15:49.960
Whether they're
internal or external,

00:15:49.960 --> 00:15:51.130
if they want to read
the data, if they

00:15:51.130 --> 00:15:52.780
want to connect to
the data, if they

00:15:52.780 --> 00:15:54.790
want to build reporting
off that data,

00:15:54.790 --> 00:15:57.500
that is something they can
do with that JDBC driver.

00:16:00.670 --> 00:16:04.020
So the type of
workloads that we expect

00:16:04.020 --> 00:16:07.017
most customers to use
with Spanner mostly

00:16:07.017 --> 00:16:08.350
fall into these four categories.

00:16:08.350 --> 00:16:09.940
And this is
something that we saw

00:16:09.940 --> 00:16:11.710
in the early access
and something

00:16:11.710 --> 00:16:14.799
that we've seen since
Spanner has launched.

00:16:14.799 --> 00:16:16.090
And the first is transactional.

00:16:16.090 --> 00:16:17.756
And this isn't really
surprising at all.

00:16:17.756 --> 00:16:20.480
It is a relational database.

00:16:20.480 --> 00:16:22.930
It has strong transactional
requirements--

00:16:22.930 --> 00:16:25.490
strong transactional
capabilities, I should say.

00:16:25.490 --> 00:16:27.969
And so we see a lot
of transactional use

00:16:27.969 --> 00:16:29.260
cases, which is not surprising.

00:16:29.260 --> 00:16:31.844
If you've got a use case where
you're selling something,

00:16:31.844 --> 00:16:33.760
you can't sell more than
you actually possess,

00:16:33.760 --> 00:16:35.350
or you want to
record transactions,

00:16:35.350 --> 00:16:37.860
you want to record
financial transactions,

00:16:37.860 --> 00:16:40.160
Spanner's a great
use case for that.

00:16:40.160 --> 00:16:42.730
And we saw this happening a
lot, especially in those cases

00:16:42.730 --> 00:16:46.690
where companies, in
order to maintain

00:16:46.690 --> 00:16:49.000
a certain size or
a certain scale,

00:16:49.000 --> 00:16:52.960
had built in a lot
of consistency logic

00:16:52.960 --> 00:16:55.420
into an application
rather than the database.

00:16:55.420 --> 00:16:58.960
And Spanner being able
to take on that logic

00:16:58.960 --> 00:17:01.960
from the application and
being able to lighten

00:17:01.960 --> 00:17:05.619
the load on development
and bug maintenance.

00:17:05.619 --> 00:17:07.540
Second, scale-out.

00:17:07.540 --> 00:17:16.630
So we often find ourselves
trying to answer the question,

00:17:16.630 --> 00:17:19.150
do I have to have a minimum
data set size to use Spanner?

00:17:19.150 --> 00:17:21.712
Does it have to be
hundreds of terabytes

00:17:21.712 --> 00:17:23.170
so I can really
efficiently use it?

00:17:23.170 --> 00:17:24.550
Absolutely not.

00:17:24.550 --> 00:17:28.089
You can start using Spanner and
start getting a really good use

00:17:28.089 --> 00:17:30.460
case out of Spanner
even at very small,

00:17:30.460 --> 00:17:32.710
say single-digit
gigabyte-sized databases.

00:17:32.710 --> 00:17:35.626
Because part of the advantage
of Spanner is not just storage.

00:17:35.626 --> 00:17:37.000
The advantage here
is that you're

00:17:37.000 --> 00:17:38.920
gaining this
availability and you're

00:17:38.920 --> 00:17:42.700
gaining the consistency of
that availability spread

00:17:42.700 --> 00:17:43.530
across a region.

00:17:43.530 --> 00:17:46.030
And so if you've got some kind
of mission critical database,

00:17:46.030 --> 00:17:48.910
if you've got something that
you absolutely have to have

00:17:48.910 --> 00:17:51.190
the highest level of
availability possible,

00:17:51.190 --> 00:17:53.680
if you've got something that
cannot go down if at all

00:17:53.680 --> 00:17:56.530
possible, and it's
got to be relational,

00:17:56.530 --> 00:18:01.420
it's got to be stored reliably,
Spanner's a great use case.

00:18:01.420 --> 00:18:03.010
And this is
something that we see

00:18:03.010 --> 00:18:05.620
a lot of customers
using where they really

00:18:05.620 --> 00:18:07.330
relied on the
availability rather

00:18:07.330 --> 00:18:11.080
than the size and
the scale in order

00:18:11.080 --> 00:18:15.050
to use for their applications,

00:18:15.050 --> 00:18:17.820
Third, and this is a very,
very common use case at Google,

00:18:17.820 --> 00:18:19.000
is a global data plan.

00:18:19.000 --> 00:18:21.550
If you've got a
user base, let's say

00:18:21.550 --> 00:18:22.960
you've got a global user base.

00:18:22.960 --> 00:18:25.856
You've got users in the States.

00:18:25.856 --> 00:18:26.980
You've got users in Europe.

00:18:26.980 --> 00:18:29.350
You've got users in Asia.

00:18:29.350 --> 00:18:32.110
And they all log into
the same application.

00:18:32.110 --> 00:18:36.140
But you're sharding along
that geographic user database.

00:18:36.140 --> 00:18:39.940
So you keep a database in each
one of those three regions,

00:18:39.940 --> 00:18:41.860
and those users are home there.

00:18:41.860 --> 00:18:44.980
When a user logs in, you've
got to figure out how

00:18:44.980 --> 00:18:49.120
to send them to the right home.

00:18:49.120 --> 00:18:50.927
So even if you're homed
in the right place,

00:18:50.927 --> 00:18:52.760
how do you know where
that person's home is?

00:18:52.760 --> 00:18:54.940
And so you've got to have
some sort of meta database

00:18:54.940 --> 00:18:57.340
to be able to
describe where to go.

00:18:57.340 --> 00:18:59.980
And that's solved by putting
that database somewhere,

00:18:59.980 --> 00:19:04.240
and two groups of people
live with bad latency.

00:19:04.240 --> 00:19:06.460
And so you can solve
that by using Spanner

00:19:06.460 --> 00:19:09.100
as an actual global
database where you get

00:19:09.100 --> 00:19:13.240
much lower latency to reads.

00:19:13.240 --> 00:19:15.710
And then finally,
database consolidation.

00:19:15.710 --> 00:19:18.880
So where this has
been really useful

00:19:18.880 --> 00:19:22.870
is if you think
of, say, a system

00:19:22.870 --> 00:19:26.440
where you need to
do some analytics

00:19:26.440 --> 00:19:30.370
and you've got your customer
transactions in a MySQL

00:19:30.370 --> 00:19:30.880
database.

00:19:30.880 --> 00:19:33.880
You've got analytics
sitting in a data warehouse

00:19:33.880 --> 00:19:36.507
and you're pulling in
log files from storage.

00:19:36.507 --> 00:19:38.590
And you want to bring them
all into a single place

00:19:38.590 --> 00:19:40.298
so you will run
analytics on top of that,

00:19:40.298 --> 00:19:41.560
you put them in reports.

00:19:41.560 --> 00:19:44.332
Where you've got data basically
in a lot of different places,

00:19:44.332 --> 00:19:45.790
and you've got to
bring it together

00:19:45.790 --> 00:19:47.415
for a single source
of truth before you

00:19:47.415 --> 00:19:49.780
can start doing reporting,
Spanner can actually

00:19:49.780 --> 00:19:51.430
replace all of that.

00:19:51.430 --> 00:19:53.080
So Spanner can be
that source of truth.

00:19:53.080 --> 00:19:56.279
It can be that location
where everything goes anyway.

00:19:56.279 --> 00:19:57.820
And you run reporting
on top of that.

00:19:57.820 --> 00:19:59.653
And so we saw that
happen a great deal, too.

00:20:02.150 --> 00:20:04.940
So after talking about
Spanner in the abstract,

00:20:04.940 --> 00:20:06.750
how does this thing
actually work?

00:20:06.750 --> 00:20:11.300
So it's pretty easy to
create a Spanner instance.

00:20:11.300 --> 00:20:14.900
I'm going to walk
through that shortly.

00:20:14.900 --> 00:20:18.560
When you create an
instance, you're basically

00:20:18.560 --> 00:20:20.990
telling the Spanner
service, what

00:20:20.990 --> 00:20:24.200
do you want your replication
topology to look like?

00:20:24.200 --> 00:20:26.700
You create a node, you
create multiple nodes--

00:20:26.700 --> 00:20:30.110
however many nodes within
a particular region--

00:20:30.110 --> 00:20:32.140
and you select that region.

00:20:32.140 --> 00:20:36.170
And then each of those nodes
has replicas across the region.

00:20:36.170 --> 00:20:39.410
And so all this data is getting
replicated automatically

00:20:39.410 --> 00:20:41.020
across the region.

00:20:41.020 --> 00:20:45.860
A few limits-- an
instance of Cloud Spanner

00:20:45.860 --> 00:20:48.350
can have up to 100
databases within it.

00:20:48.350 --> 00:20:51.430
And each database can
have 2,000 tables.

00:20:51.430 --> 00:20:53.000
But within each
table you can have

00:20:53.000 --> 00:20:55.220
an unlimited number of rows.

00:20:55.220 --> 00:21:00.070
The nodes themselves do have
a limit per storage and QPS.

00:21:02.950 --> 00:21:05.437
So what happens when
you try to do an update?

00:21:05.437 --> 00:21:07.270
What happens when you
try to write something

00:21:07.270 --> 00:21:10.140
to your Spanner instance?

00:21:10.140 --> 00:21:13.120
What happens is it
comes into the cluster,

00:21:13.120 --> 00:21:17.350
and Spanner, in some
sort of magical way,

00:21:17.350 --> 00:21:20.290
determines who is the owner
of this particular data.

00:21:20.290 --> 00:21:22.810
It is replicated
across each zone,

00:21:22.810 --> 00:21:24.340
but each particular node--

00:21:24.340 --> 00:21:26.650
or, within Spanner, there
are particular nodes

00:21:26.650 --> 00:21:27.700
that own chunks of data.

00:21:27.700 --> 00:21:29.950
They are the primary
writer of this data.

00:21:29.950 --> 00:21:31.780
And the rest of
them are replicas.

00:21:31.780 --> 00:21:34.390
And so Spanner-- again,
more or less by magic--

00:21:34.390 --> 00:21:37.640
determines who that owner is
and sends it to that owner,

00:21:37.640 --> 00:21:39.370
and they write it.

00:21:39.370 --> 00:21:42.550
We use a custom
Paxos algorithm that

00:21:42.550 --> 00:21:48.220
allows us to do consensus-based
writing that is not all voters

00:21:48.220 --> 00:21:48.970
need to agree.

00:21:48.970 --> 00:21:51.670
You just need a majority.

00:21:51.670 --> 00:21:53.650
And that gives us our
global consistency.

00:21:53.650 --> 00:21:56.120
That gives us an
ability to quickly do

00:21:56.120 --> 00:21:58.940
writes but still be consistent.

00:21:58.940 --> 00:22:02.620
There are times when we will do
a two-phase commit, especially

00:22:02.620 --> 00:22:04.774
if you're going across
nodes if there's

00:22:04.774 --> 00:22:06.940
more than one owner of the
data that you're writing.

00:22:06.940 --> 00:22:09.481
But we try to avoid that because
our Paxos algorithm actually

00:22:09.481 --> 00:22:10.640
writes the data faster.

00:22:10.640 --> 00:22:12.015
But we want to do
whatever we can

00:22:12.015 --> 00:22:15.820
to ensure that we have
global consistency.

00:22:15.820 --> 00:22:21.130
So how does that actually work
in terms of actually using it?

00:22:21.130 --> 00:22:26.660
If we consider a normal
relational database use case,

00:22:26.660 --> 00:22:28.570
let's think about these
two tables together.

00:22:28.570 --> 00:22:32.710
And so you have a singers table,
and you've got an album table.

00:22:32.710 --> 00:22:36.010
In a regular relational database
you build out each table.

00:22:36.010 --> 00:22:39.070
You make a primary key of
singer ID, primary key album ID.

00:22:39.070 --> 00:22:42.280
And then if you wanted
Albums to be a child table,

00:22:42.280 --> 00:22:45.470
you make that a composite key
or you make it a foreign key.

00:22:45.470 --> 00:22:49.120
And so you've got some foreign
key constraints on top of that.

00:22:52.120 --> 00:22:53.740
As magical-- or,
at least I believe,

00:22:53.740 --> 00:22:56.020
it's magical-- as magical
as Spanner sounds,

00:22:56.020 --> 00:22:57.670
it can't do everything
identically.

00:22:57.670 --> 00:23:00.580
There have to be trade-offs
with how it works.

00:23:00.580 --> 00:23:03.682
And the trade-offs are
in particular with how

00:23:03.682 --> 00:23:05.140
tables relate to
each other and how

00:23:05.140 --> 00:23:07.420
tables depend on each other.

00:23:07.420 --> 00:23:11.920
And so to do this,
in Spanner we do

00:23:11.920 --> 00:23:14.000
something slightly different.

00:23:14.000 --> 00:23:16.630
We actually interleave
the tables together.

00:23:16.630 --> 00:23:19.060
And this is essentially
telling Spanner,

00:23:19.060 --> 00:23:21.340
this is data that I
commonly query together.

00:23:21.340 --> 00:23:23.650
This is data I commonly
update together.

00:23:23.650 --> 00:23:28.000
Please leave them in
the same data location.

00:23:28.000 --> 00:23:30.220
And so that is what
interleaving table does.

00:23:30.220 --> 00:23:32.020
From a conceptual
point of view, this

00:23:32.020 --> 00:23:34.570
is the same thing as
two related tables

00:23:34.570 --> 00:23:36.400
with a parent-child
relationship.

00:23:36.400 --> 00:23:38.020
And you set it up the same way.

00:23:38.020 --> 00:23:43.510
So you set up the Artist table
with Artist ID, Artist Name.

00:23:43.510 --> 00:23:50.010
And then in an Album table
with Album ID, Album Name.

00:23:50.010 --> 00:23:51.430
But then you tell
Spanner, instead

00:23:51.430 --> 00:23:52.960
of saying create
a foreign key, I'm

00:23:52.960 --> 00:23:55.300
going to interleave this
table with the previous one.

00:23:55.300 --> 00:23:58.720
And I would like to-- or
not, if you don't want to--

00:23:58.720 --> 00:24:00.650
inject a dependency on this.

00:24:00.650 --> 00:24:02.980
So if you delete one, I
want you to delete the child

00:24:02.980 --> 00:24:04.154
relationships.

00:24:07.120 --> 00:24:09.210
So what does this
actually look like?

00:24:09.210 --> 00:24:12.191
If you're familiar with SQL,
it should look pretty familiar,

00:24:12.191 --> 00:24:12.690
actually.

00:24:12.690 --> 00:24:17.640
This is actually valid
DDL syntax for Spanner.

00:24:17.640 --> 00:24:21.880
So creating the Singers table
is pretty straightforward.

00:24:21.880 --> 00:24:23.960
I think that would
run on most databases.

00:24:23.960 --> 00:24:26.130
Albums, again, pretty
straightforward.

00:24:26.130 --> 00:24:28.560
And the real difference
here is that you

00:24:28.560 --> 00:24:32.230
want to interleave Singers
onto this database.

00:24:32.230 --> 00:24:34.740
And so it's going to
know that Singer ID is

00:24:34.740 --> 00:24:37.380
that primary key from the
interleaving parent table.

00:24:37.380 --> 00:24:43.330
And you've made Album ID
the primary key as well.

00:24:43.330 --> 00:24:46.050
And so one thing that's
really neat about Spanner,

00:24:46.050 --> 00:24:48.630
and is very different, I think,
from relational databases

00:24:48.630 --> 00:24:52.320
in a good way, is that
you can make table changes

00:24:52.320 --> 00:24:54.720
without taking your
database offline.

00:24:54.720 --> 00:24:58.890
You can make table changes live,
and there's no interruption

00:24:58.890 --> 00:25:00.840
in your service or performance.

00:25:00.840 --> 00:25:04.440
And so let's say that we wanted
to add an age to our Singers

00:25:04.440 --> 00:25:05.290
table.

00:25:05.290 --> 00:25:07.620
Then we just alter the table.

00:25:07.620 --> 00:25:08.970
And everyone continues.

00:25:08.970 --> 00:25:11.310
And that's now part of
that table definition.

00:25:11.310 --> 00:25:14.010
And whoever is
reading it and writing

00:25:14.010 --> 00:25:15.810
it will start using
it right away,

00:25:15.810 --> 00:25:18.710
which is pretty cool, actually.

00:25:18.710 --> 00:25:22.320
And so how does it
actually look in code?

00:25:22.320 --> 00:25:25.960
Like I said, we've got
support for Go, Java, Python,

00:25:25.960 --> 00:25:28.080
no JS right now.

00:25:28.080 --> 00:25:30.690
This is Python code, in
case you're not familiar.

00:25:30.690 --> 00:25:32.630
I'm not really a Python guy.

00:25:32.630 --> 00:25:33.630
I'm more Java.

00:25:33.630 --> 00:25:36.300
But somebody was kind enough
to write this up for me,

00:25:36.300 --> 00:25:38.790
so I'm not going to
really complain too much.

00:25:38.790 --> 00:25:41.850
But the process is relatively
similar everywhere.

00:25:41.850 --> 00:25:44.610
You can go to command line
install this library from PIP,

00:25:44.610 --> 00:25:48.690
do PIP install,
Google Cloud Library.

00:25:48.690 --> 00:25:52.560
And then in your Python file
import the Spanner library,

00:25:52.560 --> 00:25:53.840
instantiate a client.

00:25:53.840 --> 00:25:59.040
You'd say, client, I would like
an instance ID from Spanner.

00:25:59.040 --> 00:26:01.740
And then with that
instance ID, you're

00:26:01.740 --> 00:26:03.532
going to grab your
handle to your instance.

00:26:03.532 --> 00:26:05.615
And then once you grab the
handle to the instance,

00:26:05.615 --> 00:26:07.260
you say, all right,
I would like you

00:26:07.260 --> 00:26:10.470
to return me a handle to
database of whatever name

00:26:10.470 --> 00:26:12.229
so I can get a handle
on that database.

00:26:12.229 --> 00:26:13.770
And then you can
start executing SQL.

00:26:13.770 --> 00:26:17.400
And this right here, this
Execute SQL statement,

00:26:17.400 --> 00:26:20.130
is just a regular SQL query.

00:26:20.130 --> 00:26:22.796
As long it's AntiSQL 2011,
it's going to be fine.

00:26:22.796 --> 00:26:24.420
And so you can just
execute SQL queries

00:26:24.420 --> 00:26:27.750
right there, which is,
for a lot of use cases,

00:26:27.750 --> 00:26:30.449
going to be very similar to
what a lot of existing code

00:26:30.449 --> 00:26:30.990
already does.

00:26:34.726 --> 00:26:36.600
So a couple of things
that I want to touch on

00:26:36.600 --> 00:26:39.840
before I get to the demo
is, how does Spanner

00:26:39.840 --> 00:26:44.670
fit into the existing
portfolio of cloud products?

00:26:44.670 --> 00:26:48.630
I admit that this
can get confusing

00:26:48.630 --> 00:26:50.520
and/or daunting at
times, especially

00:26:50.520 --> 00:26:52.620
if you're not familiar
with how they all work.

00:26:52.620 --> 00:26:55.110
And so we do try
to spell out where

00:26:55.110 --> 00:26:57.360
we think the best
recommendations are

00:26:57.360 --> 00:26:58.570
for each of these products.

00:26:58.570 --> 00:27:00.050
So we want to start on--

00:27:00.050 --> 00:27:01.880
oh, shoot, back.

00:27:01.880 --> 00:27:04.920
We want to start on this
side with Cloud SQL.

00:27:04.920 --> 00:27:08.620
So Cloud SQL is managed
MySQL and Postgres.

00:27:08.620 --> 00:27:11.130
And so if you've got,
say, a web framework

00:27:11.130 --> 00:27:14.710
that relies on MySQL like,
say, Django, or WordPress,

00:27:14.710 --> 00:27:16.710
or what have you, that's
a really good use case.

00:27:16.710 --> 00:27:18.480
If you've got a small
data set, if you

00:27:18.480 --> 00:27:21.180
don't have high availability
requirements where

00:27:21.180 --> 00:27:25.160
you can afford some
measure of downtime,

00:27:25.160 --> 00:27:27.660
MySQL's a good fit there.

00:27:27.660 --> 00:27:35.250
If you've got a NoSQL
document store use case,

00:27:35.250 --> 00:27:39.435
this is a lot of web frameworks,
a lot of websites use this.

00:27:42.690 --> 00:27:46.590
Cloud Datastore is really
a good option for that.

00:27:46.590 --> 00:27:49.110
Because Cloud Datastore
is shared resources,

00:27:49.110 --> 00:27:52.560
you start by paying nothing,
and then you pay as you use.

00:27:52.560 --> 00:27:55.800
And so you can really
grow with Datastore.

00:27:55.800 --> 00:27:57.900
Probably the most well
known example of Datastore

00:27:57.900 --> 00:27:58.990
is Snapchat.

00:27:58.990 --> 00:28:00.980
So Snapchat started
out real small.

00:28:00.980 --> 00:28:02.280
They have gotten very big.

00:28:02.280 --> 00:28:05.050
And they've been using
Datastore the whole time.

00:28:05.050 --> 00:28:08.040
So you can really go from
very, very small to very, very

00:28:08.040 --> 00:28:09.450
large on Datastore.

00:28:09.450 --> 00:28:16.920
On the other hand, Bigtable's
more of an analytics database.

00:28:16.920 --> 00:28:20.640
Very, very high speed, low
latency, like single digit

00:28:20.640 --> 00:28:24.390
millisecond response times
for time series data,

00:28:24.390 --> 00:28:29.500
for financial analysis,
for IoT analysis.

00:28:29.500 --> 00:28:35.560
Bigtable is really particularly
well-suited for that.

00:28:35.560 --> 00:28:37.830
And then finally over
here is BigQuery.

00:28:37.830 --> 00:28:42.300
So BigQuery is a cloud
enterprise data warehouse.

00:28:42.300 --> 00:28:47.070
It is for large scale
analytics, reporting,

00:28:47.070 --> 00:28:52.530
really the end point for data
so that it can be in a place

00:28:52.530 --> 00:28:54.430
that it can be
analyzed long term.

00:28:54.430 --> 00:28:57.240
And so where Spanner
fits into all of this

00:28:57.240 --> 00:29:00.060
is Spanner is good for mission
critical databases that

00:29:00.060 --> 00:29:03.750
require high availability,
regional access.

00:29:03.750 --> 00:29:06.380
And then later on this year
when we release multi-region,

00:29:06.380 --> 00:29:09.630
it will be for this
global data plan use

00:29:09.630 --> 00:29:14.370
case that is not well suited for
the rest of these applications.

00:29:14.370 --> 00:29:18.540
And then the slide you
guys got a sneak peek at,

00:29:18.540 --> 00:29:21.270
we do understand, despite
those explanations,

00:29:21.270 --> 00:29:24.990
sometimes it can be hard
to figure out where does

00:29:24.990 --> 00:29:27.810
my particular use case lie?

00:29:27.810 --> 00:29:29.700
And so we came up
with this flowchart.

00:29:29.700 --> 00:29:32.444
It's not 100% perfect, but
it does help a great deal

00:29:32.444 --> 00:29:34.110
by making some kind
of broad assumptions

00:29:34.110 --> 00:29:35.460
and broad decisions.

00:29:35.460 --> 00:29:38.730
And so the decision
tree basically goes,

00:29:38.730 --> 00:29:40.890
your first decision,
is this structured?

00:29:40.890 --> 00:29:42.300
If it's not
structured, it really

00:29:42.300 --> 00:29:43.810
should go into object storage.

00:29:43.810 --> 00:29:45.510
So that's Cloud Storage.

00:29:45.510 --> 00:29:50.610
If you're using-- or if you
need to store unstructured data

00:29:50.610 --> 00:29:53.130
from a mobile platform,
then what you can do use

00:29:53.130 --> 00:29:57.780
is the Firebase SDK to get
to Firebase Storage, which

00:29:57.780 --> 00:29:59.880
is backed by Cloud Storage.

00:29:59.880 --> 00:30:02.460
On the other hand,
so it is structured,

00:30:02.460 --> 00:30:05.310
if you have an analytics
workload, what you really

00:30:05.310 --> 00:30:08.239
need to investigate,
is it something that's

00:30:08.239 --> 00:30:10.530
going to require updates,
it's going to require changes

00:30:10.530 --> 00:30:11.310
in low latency?

00:30:11.310 --> 00:30:13.650
In which case, it's more
of Bigtable use case.

00:30:13.650 --> 00:30:16.110
If not, then that's BigQuery.

00:30:16.110 --> 00:30:17.970
And then going down
the main branch again,

00:30:17.970 --> 00:30:22.830
if it is non-relational
data, then Datastore

00:30:22.830 --> 00:30:26.446
is a good use case if you're
not using a mobile platform.

00:30:26.446 --> 00:30:28.320
And again, if you're
using a mobile platform,

00:30:28.320 --> 00:30:30.600
Firebase Realtime DB
is a great solution

00:30:30.600 --> 00:30:33.510
for non-relational data.

00:30:33.510 --> 00:30:37.285
And then finally, if you
do have relational data,

00:30:37.285 --> 00:30:38.910
the decision comes
down to, do you need

00:30:38.910 --> 00:30:40.230
that horizontal scalability?

00:30:40.230 --> 00:30:42.840
Do you need that
higher availability?

00:30:42.840 --> 00:30:44.400
In that case, the
answer is Spanner.

00:30:44.400 --> 00:30:46.310
If not, then Cloud
SQL works pretty well.

00:30:50.470 --> 00:30:54.292
Finally, before the
demo, you've probably

00:30:54.292 --> 00:30:55.750
heard in a couple
of sessions today

00:30:55.750 --> 00:30:58.154
that partner integrations
are very important to us.

00:30:58.154 --> 00:31:00.070
We want to make sure
that partners that you're

00:31:00.070 --> 00:31:02.500
working with, software
that you're working with,

00:31:02.500 --> 00:31:04.750
works with our new
and existing products.

00:31:04.750 --> 00:31:06.955
And so launching with
a lot of partners

00:31:06.955 --> 00:31:08.080
was really important to us.

00:31:08.080 --> 00:31:11.150
And we launched with
Xplenty, Zoomdata, Looker.

00:31:15.670 --> 00:31:18.310
Pretty much anyone you're
working with or using,

00:31:18.310 --> 00:31:21.859
we are working to make sure
that our shared customers will

00:31:21.859 --> 00:31:23.650
be able to take advantage
of all the things

00:31:23.650 --> 00:31:25.820
that we create together.

00:31:25.820 --> 00:31:28.407
And so if we do
support it today,

00:31:28.407 --> 00:31:30.490
I hope you're using it and
taking advantage of it.

00:31:30.490 --> 00:31:33.560
If we're not, it's something
we're actively working on.

00:31:33.560 --> 00:31:34.060
All right.

00:31:34.060 --> 00:31:37.660
So with that, we're
going to try this demo.

00:31:42.950 --> 00:31:44.110
Cool, it works.

00:31:44.110 --> 00:31:44.610
All right.

00:31:44.610 --> 00:31:48.480
So if you're going to start
with Spanner, if you're

00:31:48.480 --> 00:31:50.410
curious about how
it goes, I really,

00:31:50.410 --> 00:31:53.525
really strongly recommend
looking at the documentation.

00:31:53.525 --> 00:31:54.900
We really did
spend a lot of time

00:31:54.900 --> 00:31:56.370
on this, a lot of iterations.

00:31:56.370 --> 00:31:58.810
And it is very, very extensive.

00:31:58.810 --> 00:32:02.220
So it goes from concepts to
quite a few how-to guides,

00:32:02.220 --> 00:32:05.850
diving into each of
the tutorials for each

00:32:05.850 --> 00:32:09.600
of the libraries, API reference,
resources, all sorts of stuff.

00:32:09.600 --> 00:32:12.330
It's really actually very, very
deep and extremely well done.

00:32:12.330 --> 00:32:18.570
So I strongly recommend
checking out the documentation.

00:32:18.570 --> 00:32:22.470
So now what I'm
going to do in my own

00:32:22.470 --> 00:32:24.360
Cloud console is
I'm actually going

00:32:24.360 --> 00:32:26.730
to create a Spanner instance.

00:32:26.730 --> 00:32:29.460
And it's bookmarked
because Spanner's awesome.

00:32:29.460 --> 00:32:31.960
And I'm going to show you just
how quickly this can be done.

00:32:31.960 --> 00:32:37.430
So let's call this next.

00:32:37.430 --> 00:32:39.220
And it's going to autofill that.

00:32:39.220 --> 00:32:41.900
We are going to select
Europe because that's

00:32:41.900 --> 00:32:43.070
where we're sitting.

00:32:43.070 --> 00:32:46.190
And let's say 10 nodes I
think is enough for here.

00:32:46.190 --> 00:32:48.380
And it'll show you how
much it costs per hour.

00:32:48.380 --> 00:32:51.360
It has some guidance around
what kind of capacity

00:32:51.360 --> 00:32:52.850
each of these nodes
are capable of

00:32:52.850 --> 00:32:54.169
and what you're signing up for.

00:32:54.169 --> 00:32:55.460
And we're going to create that.

00:32:55.460 --> 00:32:58.850
And let's see.

00:32:58.850 --> 00:33:00.860
Oh dear, it took
like three seconds.

00:33:00.860 --> 00:33:01.856
What are they doing?

00:33:01.856 --> 00:33:03.230
So anyway, this
is actually live.

00:33:03.230 --> 00:33:07.350
This is not some sort of nicety
that the console does for you.

00:33:07.350 --> 00:33:10.880
This is actually a live
status of your cluster--

00:33:10.880 --> 00:33:12.980
or instance, rather-- as
soon as you create it.

00:33:12.980 --> 00:33:14.520
So this is up and running.

00:33:14.520 --> 00:33:17.360
So those 10 nodes are now mine.

00:33:17.360 --> 00:33:21.320
But all you've done
is create a Spanner,

00:33:21.320 --> 00:33:22.650
for lack of a better term.

00:33:22.650 --> 00:33:24.360
And this is not the
same kind of thing.

00:33:24.360 --> 00:33:26.300
This is essentially a
container of databases.

00:33:26.300 --> 00:33:27.716
You haven't created
any databases.

00:33:27.716 --> 00:33:29.720
There's nothing to
query, nothing to insert.

00:33:29.720 --> 00:33:32.070
So you've got to go ahead
and create a database.

00:33:32.070 --> 00:33:35.300
And so why don't we replicate
the example from earlier?

00:33:35.300 --> 00:33:39.350
We're going to create
a small music database.

00:33:39.350 --> 00:33:41.510
And I'm just going to
create these in the console.

00:33:41.510 --> 00:33:50.480
These are all options that you
could do from the command line

00:33:50.480 --> 00:33:53.450
and from--

00:33:53.450 --> 00:33:56.400
there we go-- from
any of the libraries.

00:33:56.400 --> 00:33:59.090
And so I'm just doing this
because obviously it's

00:33:59.090 --> 00:33:59.860
a little easier.

00:33:59.860 --> 00:34:03.860
Oh, Artists, that's
not the primary key.

00:34:03.860 --> 00:34:04.670
There we go.

00:34:04.670 --> 00:34:05.690
So Artist ID.

00:34:12.400 --> 00:34:15.699
This keyboard is not
an English keyboard.

00:34:15.699 --> 00:34:17.110
So we're going to require that.

00:34:17.110 --> 00:34:21.850
We're going to add,
let's say Artist's Name.

00:34:21.850 --> 00:34:24.934
And we'll make that a string.

00:34:24.934 --> 00:34:25.600
It did it again.

00:34:30.000 --> 00:34:31.420
There, OK.

00:34:31.420 --> 00:34:33.400
And we'll require
the name as well.

00:34:33.400 --> 00:34:34.060
We'll continue.

00:34:34.060 --> 00:34:36.580
We'll make the Artist
ID the primary key.

00:34:36.580 --> 00:34:37.666
And away we go.

00:34:37.666 --> 00:34:39.040
And so now what
we're going to do

00:34:39.040 --> 00:34:43.040
is actually add the
interleaved tile table.

00:34:43.040 --> 00:34:45.310
So we'll call this Album.

00:34:45.310 --> 00:34:46.929
And we're going to
interleave this.

00:34:46.929 --> 00:34:48.699
We're going to interleave
it with Artists

00:34:48.699 --> 00:34:49.960
It's the only
choice, but you could

00:34:49.960 --> 00:34:51.418
have a drop down
of whatever tables

00:34:51.418 --> 00:34:52.639
you have in this database.

00:34:52.639 --> 00:34:54.389
We're also going to
say cascade on delete.

00:34:54.389 --> 00:34:57.080
So delete parent
child rows together.

00:34:57.080 --> 00:35:00.367
And so in this case, we
inherit Artist ID automatically

00:35:00.367 --> 00:35:02.200
because we've already
told Spanner that it's

00:35:02.200 --> 00:35:03.520
going to be interleaved.

00:35:03.520 --> 00:35:07.315
And I'm going to make
an Album ID required.

00:35:10.370 --> 00:35:15.040
And we're going to
make a Album Name.

00:35:15.040 --> 00:35:16.630
And we're going to
make that a string

00:35:16.630 --> 00:35:20.090
and say it's also
required, Done, Continue.

00:35:20.090 --> 00:35:23.140
And again, because
we're inheriting

00:35:23.140 --> 00:35:25.990
this as an interleaved table,
part of the primary key

00:35:25.990 --> 00:35:28.630
is already the
parent primary key.

00:35:28.630 --> 00:35:30.680
And it's requiring
a composite key.

00:35:30.680 --> 00:35:34.600
So we're going to select
Album ID as the second part

00:35:34.600 --> 00:35:35.590
of the composite key.

00:35:35.590 --> 00:35:36.970
And we're done.

00:35:36.970 --> 00:35:40.630
And so now we can go ahead
and create our database.

00:35:40.630 --> 00:35:45.730
And in a couple of seconds,
yep, we have tables with columns

00:35:45.730 --> 00:35:48.850
that we can start
reading and writing from.

00:35:48.850 --> 00:35:51.040
But as I sit here
and think about it,

00:35:51.040 --> 00:35:55.310
I really think we
should edit Albums

00:35:55.310 --> 00:35:57.100
because when you're
looking at Albums,

00:35:57.100 --> 00:35:59.387
you probably want to
look at a release date.

00:35:59.387 --> 00:36:00.470
It seems pretty important.

00:36:00.470 --> 00:36:10.737
So why don't we add Release
Date and make that a date type.

00:36:10.737 --> 00:36:12.320
Yes, we're going to
require that, too.

00:36:12.320 --> 00:36:16.580
Done, Save, and we've
updated our database

00:36:16.580 --> 00:36:18.230
and we haven't told
it to go offline.

00:36:18.230 --> 00:36:20.720
We haven't had a
maintenance window.

00:36:20.720 --> 00:36:26.000
We just continued
along as before.

00:36:26.000 --> 00:36:28.317
This is a pretty neat
feature of the console

00:36:28.317 --> 00:36:29.900
in that you can look
at the equivalent

00:36:29.900 --> 00:36:32.960
DDL for any of the tables.

00:36:32.960 --> 00:36:35.550
And, in fact, the entire
database has the same feature.

00:36:35.550 --> 00:36:42.700
So if we go up here, we'll
see the DDL for this database.

00:36:42.700 --> 00:36:45.710
And that's available
to you at any point.

00:36:45.710 --> 00:36:48.230
Another feature that we
have is for the database.

00:36:48.230 --> 00:36:51.680
You can look at the
monitoring on that database

00:36:51.680 --> 00:36:52.590
as you're using it.

00:36:52.590 --> 00:36:55.530
So CPU, operations per
second, throughput, and so on.

00:36:55.530 --> 00:36:57.140
This is on every
single database.

00:36:57.140 --> 00:36:59.480
And, in fact, for
the instance itself,

00:36:59.480 --> 00:37:02.510
also has that same monitoring
enabled right away.

00:37:02.510 --> 00:37:05.840
So you can look at node count
and some similar features.

00:37:05.840 --> 00:37:09.080
And now that we've been
using this for a little bit,

00:37:09.080 --> 00:37:11.270
I realize I'm massively
over provisioned.

00:37:11.270 --> 00:37:13.450
So I think 10 nodes
is way too much.

00:37:13.450 --> 00:37:15.290
Why don't we go down to five?

00:37:15.290 --> 00:37:17.300
And save that.

00:37:17.300 --> 00:37:19.470
And our Spanner
instance has continued,

00:37:19.470 --> 00:37:23.570
again without downtime,
again without end users being

00:37:23.570 --> 00:37:25.550
affected at all
because apparently we

00:37:25.550 --> 00:37:26.540
were over provisioned.

00:37:26.540 --> 00:37:29.720
And so everything
continues just fine.

00:37:29.720 --> 00:37:35.857
So now what I'm going to do
is go into a different Spanner

00:37:35.857 --> 00:37:37.940
database that was created
by one of my colleagues,

00:37:37.940 --> 00:37:40.442
conveniently called Europe
Spanner Demo Instance.

00:37:40.442 --> 00:37:42.150
And this has quite a
bit more data in it.

00:37:42.150 --> 00:37:45.170
So at least we can
do some queries here.

00:37:45.170 --> 00:37:51.487
And so this database is
backing essentially a ticketing

00:37:51.487 --> 00:37:53.570
application-- think like
Ticketmaster or something

00:37:53.570 --> 00:37:56.510
like that-- where
you have artists

00:37:56.510 --> 00:37:59.120
who go on tours to
different venues which

00:37:59.120 --> 00:38:02.170
have a certain number of
tickets and ticket categories,

00:38:02.170 --> 00:38:04.430
and users who come in and
purchase tickets, spend

00:38:04.430 --> 00:38:05.190
a certain amount of money.

00:38:05.190 --> 00:38:06.230
And so they need
accounts, and they

00:38:06.230 --> 00:38:07.354
need financial information.

00:38:07.354 --> 00:38:10.795
So a relatively classic
relational database use case.

00:38:10.795 --> 00:38:12.170
This is something
that you really

00:38:12.170 --> 00:38:13.670
need strong transactions.

00:38:13.670 --> 00:38:15.380
When people buy
tickets for concerts,

00:38:15.380 --> 00:38:19.620
they probably expect
those to actually happen.

00:38:19.620 --> 00:38:22.520
So if we look at this
database, again we've

00:38:22.520 --> 00:38:24.450
got a few indexes
on some tables,

00:38:24.450 --> 00:38:26.780
we've got some interleaved
tables, some children tables

00:38:26.780 --> 00:38:28.670
in places that you may imagine.

00:38:28.670 --> 00:38:30.570
We're using about 600 gigs.

00:38:30.570 --> 00:38:33.690
So let's do a query.

00:38:33.690 --> 00:38:36.390
And let's take advantage
of writing things down.

00:38:36.390 --> 00:38:39.090
So I'm going to take this query.

00:38:39.090 --> 00:38:42.920
And what we're looking for
is events in the Netherlands

00:38:42.920 --> 00:38:45.560
at venues that have a
significant percentage

00:38:45.560 --> 00:38:46.447
of unsold tickets.

00:38:46.447 --> 00:38:48.530
So you could imagine making
this query and saying,

00:38:48.530 --> 00:38:52.490
well, these couple of venues
for these events don't have--

00:38:52.490 --> 00:38:53.540
are largely unsold.

00:38:53.540 --> 00:38:55.915
Let's do a marketing event,
let's do a special, something

00:38:55.915 --> 00:38:56.420
like that.

00:38:56.420 --> 00:38:59.960
So let's run a query on
what's available here.

00:38:59.960 --> 00:39:06.410
And we've got two venues that
have 9,664 seats available.

00:39:06.410 --> 00:39:09.309
And that's the total
number of seats.

00:39:09.309 --> 00:39:11.600
So it looks like there are
zero sold on that first one,

00:39:11.600 --> 00:39:13.200
and the second one, same story.

00:39:13.200 --> 00:39:14.780
So they are not doing well.

00:39:14.780 --> 00:39:18.069
Whatever this event is,
it's not very popular.

00:39:18.069 --> 00:39:20.360
And with every query that
you make through the console,

00:39:20.360 --> 00:39:23.550
you can actually look
at the explain plan.

00:39:23.550 --> 00:39:26.770
And you can look
at what happened,

00:39:26.770 --> 00:39:28.700
what did the optimizer
do, how did Spanner

00:39:28.700 --> 00:39:33.090
go through the query and read
your data, and find your data?

00:39:33.090 --> 00:39:35.480
And so you can dive into that.

00:39:35.480 --> 00:39:37.100
We previously looked
at this and said,

00:39:37.100 --> 00:39:38.558
there's probably
an optimization we

00:39:38.558 --> 00:39:41.750
can make here to make
this a little bit better.

00:39:41.750 --> 00:39:46.190
We took about 3/4 of a second
to run the query and scanned

00:39:46.190 --> 00:39:47.780
nearly 15,000 rows.

00:39:47.780 --> 00:39:50.180
So what we're going
to do is actually--

00:39:50.180 --> 00:39:54.800
this is one of the features
that we have in the SQL spec--

00:39:54.800 --> 00:39:57.500
is being able to force
an index on the query.

00:39:57.500 --> 00:39:59.270
And so we're going to
force an index on--

00:40:03.180 --> 00:40:04.190
oh, dear, I've lost it.

00:40:07.854 --> 00:40:09.930
Where's Demo 2?

00:40:09.930 --> 00:40:10.430
Oh, dear.

00:40:13.380 --> 00:40:16.164
We're going to force an--

00:40:16.164 --> 00:40:17.106
on here.

00:40:22.300 --> 00:40:34.764
So this is the syntax
on, I think, event date.

00:40:39.240 --> 00:40:40.130
So here we go.

00:40:40.130 --> 00:40:44.060
We're going to try this on
the fly and see if it runs.

00:40:44.060 --> 00:40:45.210
No, it didn't.

00:40:49.510 --> 00:40:50.010
Nope.

00:40:53.264 --> 00:40:54.680
This is why you
write things down.

00:40:54.680 --> 00:40:55.630
Nope.

00:40:55.630 --> 00:40:56.590
All right.

00:40:56.590 --> 00:40:58.420
I'm terribly sorry.

00:40:58.420 --> 00:41:01.960
So if that had worked,
basically what you can do

00:41:01.960 --> 00:41:04.350
is modify your query by
looking at the explain plan.

00:41:04.350 --> 00:41:06.100
And that's something
I strongly recommend.

00:41:06.100 --> 00:41:07.810
I also strongly recommend
writing things down

00:41:07.810 --> 00:41:08.643
in the right places.

00:41:11.041 --> 00:41:12.040
I do apologize for that.

00:41:12.040 --> 00:41:13.900
That's not what I'm meant to do.

00:41:13.900 --> 00:41:16.630
But it does give you
a lot of versatility

00:41:16.630 --> 00:41:20.260
and a lot of tooling to
enable you to actually analyze

00:41:20.260 --> 00:41:20.930
your queries.

00:41:20.930 --> 00:41:23.471
So if you're having queries that
are running in ways that you

00:41:23.471 --> 00:41:26.080
don't expect, if you think
they're taking too long,

00:41:26.080 --> 00:41:28.300
if you think they're
not working or returning

00:41:28.300 --> 00:41:30.670
the right number of rows,
do look at the explain plan.

00:41:30.670 --> 00:41:32.834
Do look at what
Spanner's actually doing

00:41:32.834 --> 00:41:34.000
when you execute that query.

00:41:34.000 --> 00:41:36.333
And you can really actually
get quite a bit out of that.

00:41:36.333 --> 00:41:37.940
It might be things
like force index.

00:41:37.940 --> 00:41:40.810
It might be things like
interleaving different tables.

00:41:40.810 --> 00:41:42.880
It might be things like
adding indexes to tables.

00:41:42.880 --> 00:41:45.190
So do look at that.

00:41:45.190 --> 00:41:46.660
There is a lot of
information there

00:41:46.660 --> 00:41:52.060
in order to help you
along and to improve

00:41:52.060 --> 00:41:54.490
how you interact with Spanner.

00:41:54.490 --> 00:41:57.880
But on that note, that's it.

00:41:57.880 --> 00:42:00.932
And I'll be happy to
take any Q&amp;A. Thank you.

00:42:00.932 --> 00:42:03.878
[APPLAUSE]

00:42:07.820 --> 00:42:09.910
Yeah, if you have any
questions, could you use--

00:42:09.910 --> 00:42:11.940
are there microphones around?

00:42:11.940 --> 00:42:14.090
Oh, yeah could you use
a microphone, please?

00:42:14.090 --> 00:42:15.630
Yeah, question?

00:42:15.630 --> 00:42:18.240
