00:42:27.490 --> 00:42:28.364
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:42:28.364 --> 00:42:29.480
AUDIENCE: We usually use--

00:42:29.480 --> 00:42:30.938
PETER-MARK VERWOERD:
Oh, I'm sorry.

00:42:30.938 --> 00:42:31.956
No, I don't believe so.

00:42:34.740 --> 00:42:36.446
Next question.

00:42:36.446 --> 00:42:36.945
Right here.

00:42:42.760 --> 00:42:45.020
AUDIENCE: I have a
few simple questions.

00:42:45.020 --> 00:42:50.020
First is, does the Spanner
do backups or something,

00:42:50.020 --> 00:42:50.520
like this?

00:42:50.520 --> 00:42:51.690
I didn't saw this.

00:42:51.690 --> 00:42:52.240
PETER-MARK VERWOERD:
It's something

00:42:52.240 --> 00:42:54.220
that the Spanner team
is working on right now.

00:42:54.220 --> 00:42:56.550
It is not something that
exists in the product today.

00:42:56.550 --> 00:42:57.580
AUDIENCE: OK.

00:42:57.580 --> 00:43:01.870
The second question,
you said that Spanner

00:43:01.870 --> 00:43:04.070
don't need downtime.

00:43:04.070 --> 00:43:08.490
So how do Spanner make
[? re-indexing ?] call

00:43:08.490 --> 00:43:12.250
of a [INAUDIBLE]?

00:43:12.250 --> 00:43:15.810
When there are a lot
of updates on our DMS,

00:43:15.810 --> 00:43:21.640
so you have to re-index
the index because it's

00:43:21.640 --> 00:43:26.500
too large to data it has.

00:43:26.500 --> 00:43:31.420
Does Spanner need to re-index,
or it run automatically?

00:43:31.420 --> 00:43:34.110
PETER-MARK VERWOERD: No, I'm not
sure I got your whole question.

00:43:34.110 --> 00:43:35.690
But we don't do
automatic indexes.

00:43:35.690 --> 00:43:37.400
You can create
indexes on tables.

00:43:37.400 --> 00:43:39.120
AUDIENCE: No, I'm asking--

00:43:39.120 --> 00:43:40.245
PETER-MARK VERWOERD: Sorry.

00:43:43.240 --> 00:43:44.460
AUDIENCE: Work.

00:43:44.460 --> 00:43:47.450
I'm asking when-- so another--

00:43:47.450 --> 00:43:50.630
another way.

00:43:50.630 --> 00:43:53.530
Does the Spanner do [? vacuum ?]
out of the [INAUDIBLE]

00:43:53.530 --> 00:43:57.610
PETER-MARK VERWOERD: Oh,
auto [? vacuum. ?] OK, like--

00:43:57.610 --> 00:44:00.020
like garbage collection, and
cleaning up rows and stuff

00:44:00.020 --> 00:44:00.560
like that?

00:44:00.560 --> 00:44:02.600
Yes, yes it does.

00:44:02.600 --> 00:44:05.270
AUDIENCE: Also if indexes are
[? run out, ?] or do you know?

00:44:05.270 --> 00:44:05.810
PETER-MARK VERWOERD:
I believe so,

00:44:05.810 --> 00:44:07.420
but I'm not 100%
sure on that one.

00:44:07.420 --> 00:44:09.830
AUDIENCE: OK, thanks.

00:44:09.830 --> 00:44:11.924
PETER-MARK VERWOERD:
Any other questions?

00:44:11.924 --> 00:44:15.828
Yep, right there.

00:44:15.828 --> 00:44:17.292
AUDIENCE: Hi.

00:44:17.292 --> 00:44:19.830
[INAUDIBLE]

00:44:19.830 --> 00:44:21.164
PETER-MARK VERWOERD: I'm sorry.

