Cockroach, thank

00:45:12.641 --> 00:45:15.330
you, yes, is an open source
version of Spanner that's

00:45:15.330 --> 00:45:17.460
based off the Spanner paper.

00:45:17.460 --> 00:45:20.880
But that was not released by us.

00:45:20.880 --> 00:45:23.700
Any other questions?

00:45:23.700 --> 00:45:24.340
One more here?

00:45:29.899 --> 00:45:30.440
AUDIENCE: Hi.

00:45:30.440 --> 00:45:33.260
Thank you for the
presentation, very interesting.

00:45:33.260 --> 00:45:37.220
The question is about, so
this is globally scalable,

00:45:37.220 --> 00:45:41.240
but, at the same time,
transactional database, right?

00:45:41.240 --> 00:45:46.280
How do you with the looks of the
tables, or records, et cetera?

00:45:46.280 --> 00:45:51.200
And does it affect the
user's experience globally?

00:45:51.200 --> 00:45:54.110
PETER-MARK VERWOERD: We don't
[? lock ?] tables or rows.

00:45:54.110 --> 00:45:56.630
You can go into more detail
in the paper, which will

00:45:56.630 --> 00:45:57.990
explain it better than I can.

00:45:57.990 --> 00:46:03.290
But loosely speaking, it is
based off of time stamps.

00:46:03.290 --> 00:46:06.590
So we're pretty-- we have an
extensive time stamp system.

00:46:06.590 --> 00:46:10.940
It's mostly based on
satellites and atomic clocks

00:46:10.940 --> 00:46:12.030
in the data centers.

00:46:12.030 --> 00:46:15.050
So we have as precise
as we possibly can

00:46:15.050 --> 00:46:18.320
with an acknowledged
amount of drift time stamps

00:46:18.320 --> 00:46:20.060
for every single transaction.

00:46:20.060 --> 00:46:23.120
And they are
reconciled by a master.

00:46:23.120 --> 00:46:26.360
In a regional setup, there
is somebody who is in charge,

00:46:26.360 --> 00:46:30.080
and we elect leaders on a
relatively regular basis that

00:46:30.080 --> 00:46:32.977
is in charge of deciding
what came before

00:46:32.977 --> 00:46:33.810
and what came after.

00:46:33.810 --> 00:46:35.685
And so we have ordered
transactions that way.

00:46:35.685 --> 00:46:38.487
And so you expand that
to a global setup,

00:46:38.487 --> 00:46:40.070
and it functions the
same sort of way.

00:46:40.070 --> 00:46:41.934
But it's mostly
based off of that.

00:46:41.934 --> 00:46:43.850
But you can get a lot
more and probably better

00:46:43.850 --> 00:46:45.730
detail from the white papers.

00:46:45.730 --> 00:46:50.180
In fact, actually, as part
of the documentation website,

00:46:50.180 --> 00:46:52.822
there are links to
all the white papers

00:46:52.822 --> 00:46:54.030
that have to do with Spanner.

00:46:54.030 --> 00:46:55.740
So the original
Spanner paper that

00:46:55.740 --> 00:46:57.320
came out about 10
years ago, they

00:46:57.320 --> 00:46:59.060
did an update one this year.

00:46:59.060 --> 00:47:04.180
And there was one that
was written by Eric Brewer

00:47:04.180 --> 00:47:07.250
to specifically discuss
how Spanner handles the cap

00:47:07.250 --> 00:47:11.390
theorem, which actually goes
into a lot of what you just

00:47:11.390 --> 00:47:16.519
discussed about how to reconcile
which transaction wins when

00:47:16.519 --> 00:47:17.060
they come in.

00:47:17.060 --> 00:47:21.980
And a lot of it has to do with
having really good time stamps,

00:47:21.980 --> 00:47:24.896
but then also acknowledging
that nothing's perfect.

00:47:24.896 --> 00:47:26.270
AUDIENCE: OK, one
small question.

00:47:26.270 --> 00:47:26.820
PETER-MARK VERWOERD: Sure.

